UPDATE "News"
SET "SlugEn" = 'my-mother-called-me-home-just-to-change-a-light-bulb',
    "ShortTitleEn" = 'My Mother Called Me Home Just to Change a Light Bulb',
    "ShortDescriptionEn" = 'My mother asked me to travel more than 40 kilometers to change a flickering light bulb. Only later did I understand that the light was never the real reason she called.',
    "ContentBodyEn" = $content$
<iframe class="ql-embed" src="https://www.tiktok.com/player/v1/7684921333821590791" data-embed="iframe" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen="true" width="100%" style="aspect-ratio:9 / 16;height:auto" data-blot-formatter-unclickable-bound="true"></iframe><p><br></p><p>My mother called me home just to change a light bulb. I was annoyed because the trip was more than 40 kilometers each way, only to discover that the bulb was still working.</p><p><br></p><p>I was in the middle of work that afternoon when she called.</p><p>“Are you free? Could you come home and replace the living-room light? It keeps flickering.”</p><p>I looked at the clock and replied, “You could ask Uncle Tu next door. I’m more than 40 kilometers away.”</p><p>She was quiet for a moment. “All right… if you’re busy, never mind.”</p><p><br></p><p>I do not know why, but those words made me pick up my motorbike keys. I remained irritated throughout the ride. Changing a bulb would take less than five minutes, yet the round trip would cost me nearly an hour.</p><p><br></p><p>When I arrived, my mother was waiting at the gate. I walked into the living room and looked up. The light was on. I flipped the switch several times; everything worked normally. I removed the bulb and checked the fitting. Nothing was loose.</p><p>“There’s nothing wrong with it,” I said. “Next time, ask someone nearby. It’s a long way for me to come back for something this small.”</p><p>She simply said, “All right.”</p><p><br></p><p>As I put my things away, she asked whether I had eaten. I said I could eat later because I still had work to do. She went into the kitchen and soon returned with braised fish, vegetable soup and dipping sauce—all my favorite dishes. Two bowls had already been placed on the table.</p><p>“When did you cook all this?” I asked.</p><p>“There was a little fish left from lunch, so I warmed it up.”</p><p>But the pot was still hot. I said nothing more.</p><p><br></p><p>During dinner, she talked about things that seemed unimportant: the guava tree behind the house had begun to bear fruit, the neighbor’s cat had started sleeping by her door again, and darkness had been falling earlier lately. I ate while looking at my phone.</p><p><br></p><p>After twenty minutes, I stood up to leave. She followed me all the way to the gate.</p><p>“Will you be busy next weekend?” she asked.</p><p>“Probably. Why?”</p><p>She shook her head. “Nothing. I was only asking.”</p><p><br></p><p>Partway down the road, I remembered the light bulb, the pot of fish that was still warm, the two bowls already waiting on the table, and her words: “If you’re busy, never mind.” I pulled over.</p><p><br></p><p>When I was a child, Mother only had to call, “Come home for dinner!” and I would run home. As I grew older, my first response whenever she called became, “What do you need?” Perhaps I had asked that so many times that she had begun to believe she needed a practical reason to ask her own child to come home: a flickering bulb, a loose tap, or a phone that had suddenly “stopped working.”</p><p><br></p><p>These were tiny inconveniences that once bothered me. But perhaps the thing she wanted fixed was never the light bulb. She only needed a reason important enough for her busy child to travel 40 kilometers home.</p><p><img src="https://cafebiz.cafebizcdn.vn/zoom/700_438/2019/photo1565663659981-1565663660288-crop-15656636690861159577714.jpg" alt="A reflection on remembering Mother" height="550.0028571428571" width="879"></p><p>I turned the motorbike around.</p><p><br></p><p>When I returned, Mother was clearing the table.</p><p>“Did you forget something?” she asked, surprised.</p><p>I looked at the bulb still glowing above us and sat down.</p><p>“No. I forgot to eat another bowl of rice.”</p><p><img src="https://i.pinimg.com/736x/61/4b/ee/614bee12e62f3e5d56f8709070f784eb.jpg" alt="A meaningful image about Mother" height="576.2555555555556" width="868"></p><p>Mother smiled. “All grown up and still so forgetful.”</p><p>She went back to the kitchen for another bowl. This time I placed my phone face down on the table. She again told me about the guava tree, the neighbor’s cat, and all the things that did not seem important. I listened to every word.</p><p><br></p><p>Before leaving, I replaced the bulb even though the old one still worked.</p><p><br></p><p>A few days later, Mother called. “Nothing is wrong. I only…”</p><p>I interrupted gently: “I’ll come home for dinner this weekend.”</p><p>There was silence at the other end for a few seconds. Then she said, “All right. If you’re coming, I’ll cook fish.”</p><p><br></p><p>That was when I finally understood: as a child, I never needed a reason to go home. Somewhere along the way, my mother had begun to think she needed a reason to call me back.</p>
$content$
