UPDATE "News"
SET "SlugEn" = 'prisoners-2013-review',
    "ShortTitleEn" = 'Prisoners (2013) Review',
    "ShortDescriptionEn" = 'The 2.5-hour running time may deter some viewers, but Prisoners is an absorbing thriller with a terrific cast working at the top of their game.',
    "ContentBodyEn" = $content$
<iframe class="ql-embed" src="https://www.tiktok.com/player/v1/7683413192504036628" data-embed="iframe" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen="true" width="100%" style="aspect-ratio:9 / 16;height:auto" data-blot-formatter-unclickable-bound="true"></iframe><p class="ql-align-justify"><br></p><p class="ql-align-justify">Canadian director <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/film/denis-villeneuve" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color: rgb(134, 109, 80);">Denis Villeneuve</a>, admired for the mysterious and emotionally intricate <em>Incendies</em>, made his English-language debut with <em>Prisoners</em>: a long, brutal and often gripping crime drama. Hugh Jackman plays a father whose young daughter has been kidnapped, Jake Gyllenhaal is the detective assigned to the case, and Paul Dano is the troubled man who may hold the key. Aaron Guzikowski's original screenplay keeps several suspects and plotlines in motion before bringing them together—more or less.</p><p class="ql-align-justify">The title suggests that the film wants to be more than a conventional thriller. Its macabre flashes recall <em>The Silence of the Lambs</em> and <em>The Vanishing</em>, while its central questions concern guilt, punishment and the moral cost of “enhanced” interrogation. Beneath the police procedural lies an anxiety about what fear can persuade ordinary people to justify.</p><p class="ql-align-justify">Keller and Franklin, played by Jackman and Terrence Howard, spend Thanksgiving with their wives Grace and Nancy, played by Maria Bello and Viola Davis. Their two daughters disappear after going outside near a suspicious camper van. Detective Loki leads the search and arrests the van's driver, Alex Jones, but the police find no evidence and Alex barely responds to questioning. With no legal grounds to hold him, Loki must release him. Then Alex whispers something to Keller that makes the desperate father believe he knows more than he admits.</p><p class="ql-align-justify">Villeneuve captures the exhausting archaeology of a major investigation: vast areas are searched, old crimes resurface, and every lead reveals another layer of human misery. Loki's relentless work brings him into contact with local offenders, a decaying body and the mother of a boy who vanished decades earlier. Against that despair stands the families' rage. To them, the kidnapping feels like an act of terror, and the idea that procedure might protect the guilty becomes unbearable.</p><p class="ql-align-justify">The resulting violence is deliberately difficult to watch. The film leaves its position on torture troublingly ambiguous: is it always a moral failure that produces nothing reliable, or dirty work that can force a result? <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/film/prisoners" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color: rgb(134, 109, 80);"><em>Prisoners</em></a> condemns Keller's brutality while still inviting sympathy for his desperation. Some late revelations strain credibility as the screenplay ties off its loose ends, but Villeneuve's bleak realism, Roger Deakins's wintry images and the committed performances keep the nightmare alive.</p>
$content$
