UPDATE "News"
SET "SlugEn" = 'lesson-1-beginner-chinese-pronunciation-from-zero',
    "ShortTitleEn" = 'Lesson 1: Beginner Chinese Pronunciation — Starting from Zero',
    "ShortDescriptionEn" = 'Opportunities always favor those who are prepared. Begin learning Mandarin with a practical guide to initials, finals and tones.',
    "ContentBodyEn" = $content$
<h3><strong><u>VIDEO LESSON</u></strong></h3><p><br></p><iframe class="ql-video" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen="true" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/c7a2gpVtRIA?showinfo=0" height="436" width="872" data-blot-formatter-unclickable-bound="true"></iframe><p><br></p><h3><strong>1. HOW TO USE THIS PRONUNCIATION LESSON</strong></h3><p>This introductory lesson builds the foundations of Mandarin pronunciation. Work through one sound at a time: listen to the video, observe the position of the lips and tongue, repeat slowly, and then test the sound inside a word. A hand or a small sheet of paper held in front of the mouth can help you feel the difference between aspirated and unaspirated consonants.</p><h3><strong>2. THE INITIALS</strong></h3><p><strong>b / p:</strong> Both sounds begin with closed lips. Mandarin <strong>b</strong> is unaspirated, while <strong>p</strong> releases a strong puff of air. Compare 八 (bā, eight) with 怕 (pà, afraid).</p><p><strong>m / f:</strong> Mandarin <strong>m</strong> is the familiar nasal sound made with closed lips. For <strong>f</strong>, touch the upper teeth lightly to the lower lip and let air pass through. Examples include 妈 (mā, mother) and 飞 (fēi, fly).</p><p><strong>d / t:</strong> Place the tongue behind the upper teeth. The contrast is again aspiration: <strong>d</strong> has little escaping air, whereas <strong>t</strong> has a strong release.</p><p><strong>n / l:</strong> Both use the tongue tip near the upper teeth. Air passes through the nose for <strong>n</strong> and along the sides of the tongue for <strong>l</strong>.</p><p><strong>g / k / h:</strong> These sounds are formed toward the back of the mouth. <strong>g</strong> is unaspirated, <strong>k</strong> is strongly aspirated, and <strong>h</strong> is a friction sound similar to a firm English <em>h</em>.</p><p><strong>j / q / x:</strong> Keep the tongue forward and the lips relatively spread. <strong>j</strong> is unaspirated, <strong>q</strong> is aspirated, and <strong>x</strong> is a soft hissing sound. These initials combine with front vowels such as <strong>i</strong> and <strong>ü</strong>.</p><p><strong>zh / ch / sh / r:</strong> Curl the tongue tip slightly back. The pair <strong>zh</strong> and <strong>ch</strong> differs mainly in aspiration; <strong>sh</strong> is a retroflex hiss, while Mandarin <strong>r</strong> is voiced and should not be pronounced like a strongly rolled r.</p><p><strong>z / c / s:</strong> Keep the tongue nearer the teeth. <strong>z</strong> is unaspirated, <strong>c</strong> releases more air, and <strong>s</strong> is a clear hiss.</p><h3><strong>3. BASIC FINALS</strong></h3><p><strong>a:</strong> Open the mouth widely for a clear “ah.” <strong>o:</strong> Round the lips. <strong>e:</strong> Keep the tongue relaxed and pronounce a central vowel rather than the English letter name. <strong>i:</strong> Spread the lips slightly. <strong>u:</strong> Round and project the lips. <strong>ü:</strong> Hold the tongue as for <strong>i</strong> while rounding the lips as for <strong>u</strong>.</p><p>The compound finals in this lesson are produced as smooth glides, not as two disconnected vowels: <strong>ai</strong> moves from a to i; <strong>ei</strong> from e to i; <strong>ao</strong> from a to o; and <strong>ou</strong> from o to u. Practice with 白 (bái, white), 杯 (bēi, cup), 高 (gāo, tall) and 狗 (gǒu, dog).</p><h3><strong>4. THE FOUR TONES AND THE NEUTRAL TONE</strong></h3><p>Mandarin has four main tones plus a neutral tone. Pitch is commonly described on a five-point scale, where 1 is the lowest and 5 is the highest.</p><ol><li><strong>First tone (55):</strong> high and level. Keep the pitch steady, as in 妈 (mā, mother).</li><li><strong>Second tone (35):</strong> rise from the middle to the top, similar to the pitch of a surprised question, as in 麻 (má, hemp).</li><li><strong>Third tone (214):</strong> fall low and then rise when pronounced in isolation, as in 马 (mǎ, horse). In connected speech, it is often realized mainly as a low tone.</li><li><strong>Fourth tone (51):</strong> fall sharply and decisively from high to low, as in 骂 (mà, scold).</li><li><strong>Neutral tone:</strong> short, light and unstressed, as in the second syllable of 爸爸 (bàba, father) or 妈妈 (māma, mother).</li></ol><p><img src="https://toihoctiengtrung.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/thanh-dieu-trong-tieng-Trung.png" alt="The tones of Mandarin Chinese" height="453.1682654182372" width="861"></p><h3><strong>5. PRACTICE METHOD</strong></h3><ul><li>Listen first and avoid guessing from spelling alone.</li><li>Repeat each initial and final separately before combining them.</li><li>Use a sheet of paper to test aspiration in pairs such as b/p, d/t, g/k, j/q, zh/ch and z/c.</li><li>Record yourself saying the same syllable in all four tones and compare it with the model audio.</li><li>Practice briefly every day; accuracy should come before speed.</li></ul><iframe class="ql-embed" src="https://www.tiktok.com/player/v1/7681441033619541266" data-embed="iframe" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen="true" width="100%" style="aspect-ratio:9 / 16;height:auto" data-blot-formatter-unclickable-bound="true"></iframe><iframe class="ql-embed" src="https://www.instagram.com/p/DdMAivMAe-v/embed/captioned" data-embed="iframe" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen="true" width="100%" height="903" data-blot-formatter-unclickable-bound="true"></iframe><p><br></p><p class="ql-align-right"><strong>Compiled by Phan Thang</strong></p>
$content$
