UPDATE "News"
SET "SlugEn" = 'what-is-vibe-coding-and-how-is-it-changing-programming',
    "ShortTitleEn" = 'What Is Vibe Coding, and How Is It Changing Programming?',
    "ShortDescriptionEn" = 'Vibe Coding helps developers build software faster with AI, while raising important questions about skills, code quality and the future of the profession.',
    "ContentBodyEn" = $content$
<h1>How Is Vibe Coding Affecting Developers?</h1><p>Only a few years ago, developers spent much of their time reading documentation, searching Google, writing code line by line, debugging and browsing Stack Overflow. AI coding tools are rapidly changing that workflow.</p><p>Instead of implementing an entire program manually, a developer can describe a goal in natural language: “Create a JWT login API,” “Build a React product-list component,” or “Analyze and fix this error.” AI can generate code, scaffold projects, diagnose problems and even suggest architecture. This increasingly popular way of building software is often called <strong>Vibe Coding</strong>.</p><h2>What is Vibe Coding?</h2><p>In simple terms, Vibe Coding is a development approach in which programmers focus on describing what they want to build while AI handles much of the code generation.</p><p>The traditional process is often:</p><p><strong>Idea → Design → Code → Debug → Test → Finish</strong></p><p>With Vibe Coding, it may become:</p><p><strong>Idea → Prompt AI → Run → Review the result → Ask AI to revise → Repeat</strong></p><p>For example, instead of manually creating a login interface, a developer might ask:</p><blockquote>“Create a modern login page with Next.js and Tailwind CSS, including email, password, remember me and Google sign-in.”</blockquote><p>AI can produce most of the interface within seconds. The developer's role shifts from merely writing code toward defining requirements, making decisions and controlling the code AI produces.</p><h2>Development becomes much faster</h2><p>Speed is the clearest benefit. Work that once took hours—CRUD operations, DTOs, object mapping, validation, unit tests or basic interfaces—can be drafted in minutes. AI is particularly useful for repetitive tasks such as:</p><ul><li>creating models, repositories and CRUD APIs;</li><li>writing database migrations, regular expressions and sample data;</li><li>generating tests and API documentation;</li><li>converting formats, explaining legacy code and proposing refactors.</li></ul><p>This frees experienced developers to spend more time on business logic and system architecture. For startups and personal projects, one person can prototype an idea in days rather than requiring a full team.</p><h2>Less writing, more reading and reviewing</h2><p>When AI generates 200 lines in seconds, the question changes from “Can I write this?” to “Is what the AI wrote correct?” Those are different skills. Developers must quickly recognize faulty logic, security vulnerabilities, performance problems, architectural conflicts, technical debt, missing edge cases and maintainability risks.</p><p>In the AI era, code review becomes more important than ever.</p><h2>Beginners can build products sooner</h2><p>Vibe Coding lowers the barrier to entry. Someone with basic HTML, JavaScript or database knowledge can use AI to create a website, management tool or simple SaaS product while learning along the way. AI can write code and explain dependency injection, JWT validation or query optimization at the same time.</p><p>Used well, it becomes an always-available programming tutor.</p><h2>The risk: running code you do not understand</h2><p>A common workflow is: AI writes code, the program fails, the error is pasted back into AI, AI revises it, and the loop continues until the application appears to work. The developer may still have no idea why it works.</p><p>That weakness becomes dangerous as a system grows. Modules become tightly coupled, queries slow down, authentication develops gaps, business logic is duplicated and each change breaks something else. Knowing how to ask AI for code is not the same as knowing how to engineer software.</p><h2>Junior developers may feel the greatest impact</h2><p>Entry-level developers traditionally begin with small bugs, forms, validation, CRUD endpoints and interface adjustments—the same tasks AI handles well. Employers may therefore expect juniors to demonstrate stronger fundamentals: HTTP, authentication versus authorization, indexes, transactions, REST design, caching, query performance and real-world debugging.</p><p>AI does not make those foundations less important. It makes them more important because someone must verify the generated output.</p><h2>Senior developers remain essential</h2><p>AI can generate a good function or module, but large systems still require human judgment: dividing domains, assigning service responsibilities, modeling data, choosing caching strategies, planning capacity, balancing consistency and availability, designing permissions, managing migrations and keeping a system maintainable for years.</p><p>That work is not merely coding. It is <strong>software engineering</strong>. Experienced developers use AI effectively because they know what to ask, what a credible answer looks like and when a proposed solution is poor.</p><h2>Value shifts from writing code to solving problems</h2><p>AI separates programming from the physical act of typing code. A person can generate a large amount of code without solving the right problem, while a strong engineer may write less and make better decisions.</p><p>The valuable workflow increasingly becomes:</p><p><strong>Understand the problem → analyze requirements → design a solution → delegate to AI → review → test → improve.</strong></p><h2>Prompting is a technical communication skill</h2><p>“Build a booking feature” will usually produce a generic result. A useful request specifies the framework, database, overlap rules, transaction guarantees, asynchronous queries and concurrency constraints. The real skill is not memorizing magic prompts; it is describing a technical problem precisely. Doing that still requires a deep understanding of the system.</p><h2>AI is a collaborator, not the driver</h2><p>Vibe Coding should not mean “AI will code instead of me.” A healthier principle is: “AI will remove routine work so I can focus on more important decisions.” Let AI produce boilerplate, tests, documentation, refactoring suggestions and prototypes. Keep architecture, business rules, security, data and final decisions under human control.</p><p>The hardest part of software development has never been typing quickly. It is understanding the real problem and building a system that works reliably in the real world.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Vibe Coding is changing software development faster than many expected. Repetitive work will be automated, prototypes will arrive sooner and more people will be able to build useful applications.</p><p>That does not make programming knowledge obsolete. When AI can generate thousands of lines in minutes, the ability to understand code, evaluate it and make sound technical decisions becomes even more valuable. Vibe Coding may make software easier to write, but good software still requires people who understand what they are building.</p>
$content$
WHERE "NewsId" = 'vibe-coding-la-gi-no-dang-thay-doi-nghe-lap'
  AND "FlagActive" = TRUE
  AND "ShortTitleEn" = ''
  AND "ShortDescriptionEn" = ''
  AND "ContentBodyEn" = '';

