UPDATE "News"
SET "SlugEn" = '11-stunning-ha-giang-landscapes-that-capture-vietnams-northern-highlands',
    "ShortTitleEn" = '11 Stunning Ha Giang Landscapes in Vietnam''s Northern Highlands',
    "ShortDescriptionEn" = 'From limestone peaks and steep mountain passes to misty villages and terraced fields, these 11 destinations reveal the remarkable natural and cultural beauty of Ha Giang.',
    "ContentBodyEn" = $content$
<p class="ql-align-justify">Ha Giang is impressive throughout the year. The warm rainy season lasts from April to October, although travelers should avoid July and August when heavy rain brings the highest risk of landslides. The dry season runs from November to March, with December and January often becoming quite cold in the mountains.</p><h2 class="ql-align-justify"><span style="color: rgb(0, 153, 204);">1</span></h2><h2 class="ql-align-justify">The terraced fields of Hoang Su Phi</h2><p class="ql-align-justify"><a href="https://mia.vn/cam-nang-du-lich/hoang-su-phi-ngo-ngang-ve-dep-ruong-bac-thang-mua-lua-chin-3602" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color: rgb(240, 79, 36);">Hoang Su Phi</a> remains one of Vietnam's less crowded and more unspoiled destinations. During the rice harvest, its high mountain terraces turn into some of the country's most beautiful golden landscapes. Walking between ethnic minority villages also offers a chance to learn how local communities have cultivated these slopes for generations.</p><p class="ql-align-center"><img src="https://mia.vn/media/uploads/blog-du-lich/canh-dep-ha-giang-1-1722246208.jpg" alt="Terraced rice fields in Hoang Su Phi" height="552.12" width="856"><em>Hoang Su Phi is famous for its terraced fields during harvest season. Photo: indochinatravelpackages</em></p><h2 class="ql-align-justify"><span style="color: rgb(0, 153, 204);">2</span></h2><h2 class="ql-align-justify">Quan Ba Heaven Gate, an ideal place to chase the clouds</h2><p class="ql-align-justify"><a href="https://mia.vn/cam-nang-du-lich/lac-troi-giua-chon-tien-canh-nhan-gian-tai-cong-troi-quan-ba-ha-giang-3406" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color: rgb(240, 79, 36);">Quan Ba Heaven Gate</a> lies at roughly 1,500 meters above sea level between mountain peaks. From here, visitors can look across limestone ranges and terraced fields toward the distinctive Fairy Bosom twin mountains emerging through the mist. Sunrise is especially rewarding.</p><p class="ql-align-center"><img src="https://mia.vn/media/uploads/blog-du-lich/canh-dep-ha-giang-2-1722246211.jpg" alt="Quan Ba Heaven Gate overlooking the twin mountains" height="471.35" width="857"><em>Quan Ba Pass overlooks the Fairy Bosom twin mountains. Photo: localvietnam</em></p><h2 class="ql-align-justify"><span style="color: rgb(0, 153, 204);">3</span></h2><h2 class="ql-align-justify">The historic character of Dong Van Old Quarter</h2><p class="ql-align-justify"><a href="https://mia.vn/cam-nang-du-lich/den-pho-co-dong-van-de-trai-nghiem-ro-cai-van-hoa-ha-giang-binh-di-3413" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color: rgb(240, 79, 36);">Dong Van Old Quarter</a> began taking shape in the early twentieth century. Today, around forty century-old houses preserve the area's architectural character. On weekends the market comes alive with trade, folk songs, dances and festivals shared by the region's ethnic communities.</p><p class="ql-align-center"><img src="https://mia.vn/media/uploads/blog-du-lich/canh-dep-ha-giang-3-1722246213.jpg" alt="Dong Van market" height="473.55" width="861"><em>Dong Van's Sunday market is the old quarter's liveliest weekly event. Photo: localvietnam</em></p><h2 class="ql-align-justify"><span style="color: rgb(0, 153, 204);">4</span></h2><h2 class="ql-align-justify">Sa Phin Valley and the Hmong King's Palace</h2><p class="ql-align-justify">About twenty minutes from Dong Van, Sa Phin is home to the <a href="https://mia.vn/cam-nang-du-lich/dinh-thu-vua-meo-dinh-thu-ho-vuong-dia-danh-bi-an-va-doc-dao-3411" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color: rgb(240, 79, 36);">Hmong King's Palace</a>, also known as the Vuong family mansion. Built on a turtle-shaped hill and recognized as an architectural heritage site in 1993, the complex blends Chinese and European influences. Visitors can also explore the valley, village and Sa Phin market.</p><p class="ql-align-center"><img src="https://mia.vn/media/uploads/blog-du-lich/canh-dep-ha-giang-4-1722246216.jpg" alt="The Hmong King's Palace in Sa Phin" height="483.75" width="860"><em>The Hmong King's Palace in Sa Phin Valley. Photo: Ngoc Hai</em></p><h2 class="ql-align-justify"><span style="color: rgb(0, 153, 204);">5</span></h2><h2 class="ql-align-justify">Sung La, a flower in the stone plateau</h2><p class="ql-align-justify"><a href="https://mia.vn/cam-nang-du-lich/thung-lung-sung-la-ha-giang-bong-hoa-no-giua-cao-nguyen-da-3408" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color: rgb(240, 79, 36);">Sung La</a> is a peaceful Hmong valley known for broad fields and seasonal flowers. Lung Cam village is home to Lo Lo, Hmong and Han communities, local handicrafts and traditional earthen houses. One of those houses became famous nationwide after appearing in the Vietnamese film <em>The Story of Pao</em>.</p><p class="ql-align-center"><img src="https://mia.vn/media/uploads/blog-du-lich/canh-dep-ha-giang-5-1722246218.jpg" alt="Fields and flowers in Sung La Valley" height="567.6424709841501" width="852"><em>Flowers grow among the rice and corn fields of Sung La. Photo: localvietnam</em></p><h2 class="ql-align-justify"><span style="color: rgb(0, 153, 204);">6</span></h2><h2 class="ql-align-justify">Lung Cu Flag Tower at Vietnam's northern frontier</h2><p class="ql-align-justify">Lung Cu marks Vietnam's far north and is a symbol of national pride. The 33.15-meter <a href="https://mia.vn/cam-nang-du-lich/cot-co-lung-cu-dau-an-thieng-thieng-noi-dia-dau-to-quoc-3458" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color: rgb(240, 79, 36);">flag tower</a> provides a sweeping view over the mountains. Its octagonal body bears Dong Son drum motifs, while the 54-square-meter national flag represents Vietnam's 54 ethnic groups.</p><p class="ql-align-center"><img src="https://mia.vn/media/uploads/blog-du-lich/canh-dep-ha-giang-6-1722246220.jpg" alt="Lung Cu Flag Tower" height="636.75" width="849"><em>Lung Cu Flag Tower stands at Vietnam's northernmost frontier. Photo: vncarrentals</em></p><h2 class="ql-align-justify"><span style="color: rgb(0, 153, 204);">7</span></h2><h2 class="ql-align-justify">The winding Ma Pi Leng Pass</h2><p class="ql-align-justify">At around 1,500 meters between Dong Van and Meo Vac, <a href="https://mia.vn/cam-nang-du-lich/chinh-phuc-deo-ma-pi-leng-tu-dai-dinh-deo-cua-vung-nui-dat-bac-3400" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color: rgb(240, 79, 36);">Ma Pi Leng Pass</a> is considered one of Vietnam's most spectacular mountain roads. The route makes travelers feel tiny against the surrounding peaks and reveals the turquoise Nho Que River winding far below.</p><p class="ql-align-center"><img src="https://mia.vn/media/uploads/blog-du-lich/canh-dep-ha-giang-7-1722246222.jpg" alt="The winding Ma Pi Leng Pass" height="646.5" width="862"><em>The road across Ma Pi Leng offers spectacular views of Ha Giang. Photo: tomorrowtravel</em></p><h2 class="ql-align-justify"><span style="color: rgb(0, 153, 204);">8</span></h2><h2 class="ql-align-justify">The quiet town of Pho Bang</h2><p class="ql-align-justify"><a href="https://mia.vn/cam-nang-du-lich/kham-pha-thi-tran-pho-bang-co-kinh-an-minh-trong-vung-cao-ha-giang-3528" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color: rgb(240, 79, 36);">Pho Bang</a> has fewer than 3,000 residents, mainly Hmong and Han Chinese. Its earthen houses and yin-yang tiled roofs create a distinctive streetscape, while roses and buckwheat flowers bloom in the surrounding valley.</p><p class="ql-align-center"><img src="https://mia.vn/media/uploads/blog-du-lich/canh-dep-ha-giang-8-1722246225.jpg" alt="Traditional architecture in Pho Bang" height="651.33375" width="867"><em>The peaceful town preserves distinctive ethnic architecture. Photo: @alichcutan</em></p><h2 class="ql-align-justify"><span style="color: rgb(0, 153, 204);">9</span></h2><h2 class="ql-align-justify">The whispering pines of Yen Minh</h2><p class="ql-align-justify">Often called a miniature Da Lat, <a href="https://mia.vn/cam-nang-du-lich/phuot-trai-nghiem-cung-duong-rung-thong-yen-minh-reo-vui-3630" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color: rgb(240, 79, 36);">Yen Minh pine forest</a> is beloved for its tall trees and sweeping green meadows. Travelers can camp beneath the pines and wake to early morning mist at sunrise.</p><p class="ql-align-center"><img src="https://mia.vn/media/uploads/blog-du-lich/canh-dep-ha-giang-9-1722246227.jpg" alt="Tall pines in Yen Minh" height="850" width="850"><em>Pine trees rise high above Yen Minh. Photo: FB Liberzy</em></p><h2 class="ql-align-justify"><span style="color: rgb(0, 153, 204);">10</span></h2><h2 class="ql-align-justify">The natural formations of Lung Khuy Cave</h2><p class="ql-align-justify">Discovered in 2015, <a href="https://mia.vn/cam-nang-du-lich/kham-pha-dong-lung-khuy-muon-mau-muon-ve-de-nhat-dong-ha-giang-3587" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color: rgb(240, 79, 36);">Lung Khuy Cave</a> quickly became a source of local pride. The cave extends for more than a kilometer beneath a vast rock ceiling and contains an extraordinary variety of ancient limestone stalactites and stalagmites.</p><p class="ql-align-center"><img src="https://mia.vn/media/uploads/blog-du-lich/canh-dep-ha-giang-10-1722246229.jpg" alt="Limestone formations in Lung Khuy Cave" height="569.1062499999999" width="851"><em>Lung Khuy is one of the Dong Van Karst Plateau's most beautiful caves. Photo: localhagiang</em></p><h2 class="ql-align-justify"><span style="color: rgb(0, 153, 204);">11</span></h2><h2 class="ql-align-justify">Wild Noong Lake in the mountains</h2><p class="ql-align-justify"><a href="https://mia.vn/cam-nang-du-lich/ho-noong-chon-bong-lai-giua-long-ha-giang-3847" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color: rgb(240, 79, 36);">Noong Lake</a> is a natural freshwater lake on the Tay Con Linh range. Underground water flowing through caves connected to the Lo River keeps it from drying out. Primeval forest and dramatic rock mountains surround its calm waters.</p><p class="ql-align-center"><img src="https://mia.vn/media/uploads/blog-du-lich/canh-dep-ha-giang-11-1722246231.jpg" alt="The beauty of Noong Lake" height="491.35875" width="849"><em>The captivating beauty of Noong Lake. Photo: vietnam-destinations</em></p><p class="ql-align-justify">Save these destinations for a meaningful journey through Ha Giang. Traveling by motorbike can be an unforgettable way to experience the province, but always check the weather, road conditions and your riding ability before setting out.</p>
$content$
