UPDATE "News"
SET "SlugEn" = 'discover-the-famous-flavors-of-phu-ly-steamed-rice-rolls',
    "ShortTitleEn" = 'Discover the Famous Flavors of Phu Ly Steamed Rice Rolls',
    "ShortDescriptionEn" = 'Phu Ly bánh cuốn pairs delicate steamed rice sheets with charcoal-grilled pork, crisp pickles and warm dipping sauce, creating one of northern Vietnam''s most distinctive local dishes.',
    "ContentBodyEn" = $content$
<p class="ql-align-justify"><strong>Phu Ly steamed rice rolls, or bánh cuốn Phủ Lý, embody the simple yet captivating flavors of the former Ha Nam area. Silky rice sheets, fragrant grilled pork and a carefully balanced warm dipping sauce give the dish its unmistakable character. For travelers interested in local food and regional culture, it is an experience well worth seeking out.</strong></p><h2 class="ql-align-justify"><strong>1. An introduction to Phu Ly bánh cuốn</strong></h2><p class="ql-align-justify">This specialty has been part of everyday life in Phu Ly for generations. More than a familiar breakfast, it is a source of culinary pride at the southern gateway to Hanoi. Local cooks transform simple ingredients—fragrant rice, pork and fresh herbs—into a dish with a strong regional identity.</p><p class="ql-align-justify"><img src="https://sunparadiseland.com/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fsun-ecommerce-cdn.azureedge.net%2Fecommerce%2Fservice-sites%2Fasset%2FSunParadiseLandHaNam%2Fgoogle-doc%2Fpost_id_12231%2FAD_4nXczK0eXrZH7H--WTqJZUMzH0PdnV5Y5QSq5i-yVgfCiV1Y3QLIA2J714BQUmmDBFR1DYW3i8e15xAEeX0mPOyLuSIuwplSGhaLJnD_CIusWXi0A3m_QWP2ZXAZLaEkX08K3ZHExVPlSVYbhXrsfnYFK2czz7HcJKskZlBO0kYCJAtz5hto.webp&amp;w=1200&amp;q=80" alt="Delicious Phu Ly steamed rice rolls"></p><p class="ql-align-justify"><em>Phu Ly bánh cuốn is delicate, fragrant and deeply satisfying. Photo: collected</em></p><p class="ql-align-justify">Unlike the styles found in Thanh Tri or Bac Ninh, Phu Ly's rice sheets are exceptionally thin, smooth and translucent. They are served with aromatic grilled pork and hot dipping sauce, a combination that draws food lovers to the city.</p><h2 class="ql-align-justify"><strong>2. What makes Phu Ly bánh cuốn special?</strong></h2><p class="ql-align-justify"><strong>Fragrant tám xoan rice</strong></p><p class="ql-align-justify">The signature texture begins with small-grained tám xoan rice. Naturally sweet and moderately sticky, it produces tender sheets with enough elasticity to remain intact. The rice is soaked for three to four hours, wet-milled into a fine batter and carefully filtered through layers of cloth. Each step affects the clarity, softness and resilience of the finished sheet.</p><p class="ql-align-justify"><img src="https://sunparadiseland.com/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fsun-ecommerce-cdn.azureedge.net%2Fecommerce%2Fservice-sites%2Fasset%2FSunParadiseLandHaNam%2Fgoogle-doc%2Fpost_id_12231%2FAD_4nXdrwp1Hz8HH4k1iy7ZPGph8DlXA-cQO0PjbvE2KpiwkZJfaYeyPwovc3R1OA9c-0smCxr1e29mCxZb6mgDRc5JYTtml2Pem32yO4n6dvPMMOmAd6giMwFOSqdLzBzRecJ0NpTpt3UPaNSI6fbVFxtWZNlsQLy9wHP-fGmVnkXusO320XEA.webp&amp;w=1200&amp;q=80"></p><p class="ql-align-justify"><em>The rolls are traditionally made from fragrant tám xoan rice. Photo: collected</em></p><p class="ql-align-justify"><strong>Shallots fried in pork fat</strong></p><p class="ql-align-justify">Another essential detail is the topping of golden shallots fried in pure pork fat rather than ordinary cooking oil. Freshly steamed sheets are quickly coated with the fragrant fat and shallots, giving them shine and richness without becoming heavy.</p><p class="ql-align-justify"><img src="https://sunparadiseland.com/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fsun-ecommerce-cdn.azureedge.net%2Fecommerce%2Fservice-sites%2Fasset%2FSunParadiseLandHaNam%2Fgoogle-doc%2Fpost_id_12231%2FAD_4nXf4mE3C6UeLF5AZD_akrV7SsvrWL5i1HWlo5Rp0Y9iEd8SauRDCwXnSbAGtsE7gKesrSBhxGNIm6lPwGbQDflo8oIUeKkZKZFF4VEouPvwjn-PlmFnw1LDqJjPVQr-gqXGwRPu5eSSskteHzotLV0ziOspmVwK51HUaR5SGmHlQtnt0OtE.webp&amp;w=1200&amp;q=80" alt="Golden fried shallots on Phu Ly rice rolls"></p><p class="ql-align-justify"><strong>Charcoal-grilled pork and pickles</strong></p><p class="ql-align-justify">Pork belly or shoulder is sliced, marinated with shallot, pepper, fish sauce and a little honey, then grilled over glowing charcoal until golden outside and tender within. Pickled cucumber and carrot add a crisp, lightly sour contrast, while lettuce, perilla and mint bring freshness.</p><p class="ql-align-justify"><img src="https://sunparadiseland.com/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fsun-ecommerce-cdn.azureedge.net%2Fecommerce%2Fservice-sites%2Fasset%2FSunParadiseLandHaNam%2Fgoogle-doc%2Fpost_id_12231%2FAD_4nXe0TBLZTsXkjNZEkzk7cI-beUuuxhyJli54bnd5xwROaNrUJs2vCjlJwVkv6VYgBS7swms27A7ohxKGY2COhkXaraESU12u2oUFv55X1tPH-jMPDbR5b2JoYG9bAW3uH1SDwxKSuLTLRRUWLs6WUzP-ou56scTAUAS28tEvYNfF9VOFZg8.webp&amp;w=1200&amp;q=80" alt="Hot grilled pork served with Phu Ly rice rolls"></p><p class="ql-align-justify"><strong>A carefully mixed warm dipping sauce</strong></p><p class="ql-align-justify">The dipping sauce is the soul of the dish. Good fish sauce is balanced with sugar, vinegar and water or coconut water; some cooks add a little bone broth for gentle sweetness. Pepper, chili and minced garlic are added while the sauce is warm, allowing the aromas to blend. Each shop guards its own proportions, but the ideal result is clear, mild and balanced rather than overpowering.</p><p class="ql-align-justify"><img src="https://sunparadiseland.com/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fsun-ecommerce-cdn.azureedge.net%2Fecommerce%2Fservice-sites%2Fasset%2FSunParadiseLandHaNam%2Fgoogle-doc%2Fpost_id_12231%2FAD_4nXeWqyGSSOdfeh8icJWg3zmUUbNh9yTN-pX0_i0kU9vkVGRPrmdgnTmt0A0-7I1LMHg0ForZ4BDSuAkUMSqNO5GTx6BVoleDiC4gEyBQpNiLA9mgC-k1Hu5XrG4zBA6GcxCIGNSh1qo5t_B9K6ZDoLMr1QaWhWo2LQ4XU9FhLuxdoT3sLQk.webp&amp;w=1200&amp;q=80" alt="Warm dipping sauce for Phu Ly rice rolls"></p><h2 class="ql-align-justify"><strong>3. How traditional Phu Ly bánh cuốn is made</strong></h2><h3 class="ql-align-justify"><strong>Preparing the ingredients</strong></h3><p class="ql-align-justify">Choose fresh, uniform tám xoan rice; firm dry shallots; pork with a balanced ratio of lean meat and fat; and young, crisp herbs. High-quality fish sauce, vinegar, sugar, garlic, chili, pepper and honey complete the flavor.</p><h3 class="ql-align-justify"><strong>Steaming thin, unbroken sheets</strong></h3><p class="ql-align-justify">After soaking and grinding, the batter is filtered, diluted to the right consistency and rested for 15–20 minutes. A very thin layer is spread over taut cloth above boiling water, covered and steamed for about 30–40 seconds. When translucent, it is lifted gently with a bamboo stick, coated with shallot oil and folded onto a plate.</p><h3 class="ql-align-justify"><strong>Grilling the pork</strong></h3><p class="ql-align-justify">Slices about half a centimeter thick are marinated for one to two hours, then grilled over medium charcoal heat and turned frequently. The smoke gives the meat a fragrance that other cooking methods cannot reproduce.</p><h3 class="ql-align-justify"><strong>Mixing the local-style dipping sauce</strong></h3><p class="ql-align-justify">A useful starting ratio is three parts fish sauce, two parts water or coconut water and one part vinegar, with sugar adjusted to taste. Mix it warm, then add garlic, chili and pepper. Make small batches so the sauce remains fresh and clear.</p><h2 class="ql-align-justify"><strong>4. Four places to try Phu Ly bánh cuốn</strong></h2><h3 class="ql-align-justify"><strong>Hung Nu</strong></h3><p class="ql-align-justify">At 217C Truong Chinh, Hung Nu serves thin hand-steamed rolls, seasoned pork filling, grilled skewers and an unusual crispy rice-paper accompaniment. A serving costs about VND 30,000, and the spacious shop has car parking.</p><h3 class="ql-align-justify"><strong>Minh Ngoc Nhu Y</strong></h3><p class="ql-align-justify">Located in Alley 198 Truong Chinh, this long-running breakfast shop prepares each serving to order. Expect smooth rice sheets, aromatic filling, balanced sweet-and-sour sauce and lean grilled pork. It often sells out early; a serving is around VND 40,000.</p><h3 class="ql-align-justify"><strong>Phuong Thanh</strong></h3><p class="ql-align-justify">This traditional shop at 73 Nguyen Viet Xuan is known for evenly steamed rolls, a slightly sweet house sauce and charcoal-scented pork. The small, clean space suits a quick meal, with servings around VND 35,000.</p><h3 class="ql-align-justify"><strong>Leo Quan</strong></h3><p class="ql-align-justify">One of the few late-night choices, Leo Quan at 160 Truong Chinh offers soft rolls, well-seasoned filling, grilled pork and an easy-to-enjoy dipping sauce in a casual, airy setting.</p><h2 class="ql-align-justify"><strong>5. Combining food and sightseeing</strong></h2><p class="ql-align-justify">Begin around 7 a.m. with bánh cuốn in Phu Ly, then visit Ba Danh Pagoda and Ngoc Mountain. Lunch might include another local specialty such as Vu Dai braised fish. In the afternoon, families can explore Sun World Ha Nam and continue sampling regional dishes.</p><p class="ql-align-justify">Every plate of Phu Ly bánh cuốn tells a small story about the food culture of northern Vietnam: translucent rice sheets, smoky pork, fresh herbs and warm dipping sauce brought together through generations of local craft.</p>
$content$
